  "opening": {
    "en": [
      {
        "title": "Vehicle Setup for Beginners",
        "body": "Start with balanced kart/character combinations (Mario, Toad, or Yoshi with a medium-weight kart) to develop steering feel. Avoid heavyweight combinations (Bowser, Waluigi) until drift timing is comfortable—heavier builds drift wider and punish corner entry errors more severely. Unlock and test glider upgrades early; open-world elevation changes make aerial gliding transitions a recurring skill throughout the campaign."
      },
      {
        "title": "Open-World Navigation Basics",
        "body": "The open world connects all race courses via navigable roads and pathways. Before entering cups, drive freely between venues to memorize the map layout and discover bonus coins and hidden shortcuts. GPS markers appear on the minimap for undiscovered locations; collecting all bonus items in a region before cup entry provides a currency advantage for vehicle upgrades."
      }
    ]
  },
  "midGame": {
    "en": [
      {
        "title": "Item Strategy in Competitive Races",
        "body": "Item management separates intermediate from casual players. Hold a defensive item (banana, shell) in the trailing slot while carrying a speed item in the lead slot whenever possible. In the final lap, save mushrooms for the last stretch—post-finish line boost extends lead over close competitors. Avoid using the boomerang flower offensively in tight groups; the recovery animation leaves you vulnerable to chain hits."
      }
    ]
  },
  "endGame": {
    "en": [
      {
        "title": "Mastering Online Competitive Play",
        "body": "High-tier online play rewards consistent racing over aggressive item usage. Calculate risk-to-reward for each item use: a missed forward throw costs your item slot; a successful backwards throw disrupts two opponents. Study the global leaderboard courses to understand high-frequency shortcuts, and practice the difficult ultra-turbo corners that separate top-ranked racers from the mid-tier bracket."
      }
    ]
  },
  "mistakes": {
    "en": [
      "Braking in corners instead of drifting—braking kills speed more severely than a bad drift; a drifted corner with no boost is still faster than a braked corner without drift.",
      "Using items immediately rather than holding for optimal moments—patience with item timing produces significantly better race outcomes than immediate item expenditure.",
      "Not using anti-gravity or underwater sections to experiment with alternate lines—these physics zones offer genuinely different optimal paths from the standard surface.",
      "Neglecting the open-world bonus coin economy—vehicle upgrades from coins provide measurable stats improvements that compound over multiple cup entries.",
      "Focusing only on boost and ignoring slipstreaming—following a rival kart closely activates a slipstream speed boost that overtakes even boosted opponents on long straights."
